---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Icon from '../Icon.astro';
import CubbyStory from './CubbyStory.astro';
import CubbyHomeEvolution from './CubbyHomeEvolution.astro';
import CubbyDecisions from './CubbyDecisions.astro';
import CubbyIndex, { type IndexItem } from './CubbyIndex.astro';
import ScreenshotDialog from '../ScreenshotDialog.astro';
import dashboard from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/dashboard.webp';
import dashboardRecent from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/dashboard-recent.webp';
import tasks from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/tasks.webp';
import limits from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/limits.webp';
import market from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/market.webp';
import finance from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/finance.webp';
import expiry from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/expiry.webp';
import stock from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/stock.webp';
import gymWorkout from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/gym-workout.webp';
import coffee from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/coffee.webp';
import meals from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/meals.webp';
import illness from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/illness.webp';
import journalRecap from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/journal-recap.webp';
import growingUp from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/growing-up.webp';
import enrichment from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/enrichment.webp';
import screentimer from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/screentimer.webp';
import schedule from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/schedule.webp';
import sleep from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/sleep.webp';
import jobs from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/jobs.webp';
import CubbyLogo from '$lib/assets/cubby-logo.webp';

const groups = [
	{
		label: 'Routines',
		note: 'recurring upkeep, limits, and schedules',
		intro:
			'Once I could check when something was last done, I wanted the same record for the routines we repeated as a family.',
		items: [
			{
				src: tasks,
				alt: 'Cubby tasks',
				headline: 'When did I last do this?',
				paragraphs: [
					"I started with towels because I kept forgetting when I'd last washed them. A dated log was enough for that, but my kid's nasal spray needed more care. The routine changed from daily to every two days, then three, while we also tried to track congestion. Our few Telegram messages left us with very little to tell the doctor.",
					'Each entry now keeps the interval that applied when we logged it. Changing the routine leaves that history intact, so I can look back and see what we were doing at the time.',
				],
			},
			{
				src: limits,
				alt: 'Cubby limits',
				headline: 'On pace, or already over',
				paragraphs: [
					'Screen time kept growing, and I wanted to keep an eye on soda and beer too. I gave each habit a weekly limit and a daily pace, so I could check how the week was going while there was still time to do something about it.',
					"Drinks needed unit conversion because a McDonald's small Coke and a can contain different amounts. Seeing the week's allowance nearly gone has talked me out of a drink more than once.",
				],
			},
			{
				src: screentimer,
				alt: 'Cubby screen timer',
				headline: 'The same timers, by hand, every day',
				paragraphs: [
					'The screen timer gave me a way to connect that weekly limit to what we actually did. YouTube countdowns had fixed durations and sounds, so I built saved profiles with AI voices and ticking sounds that my kid could listen to with his eyes closed.',
					'When a timer finishes, it logs against the limit automatically. That saved a second entry and gave the two features a reason to live in the same app.',
				],
			},
			{
				src: enrichment,
				alt: 'Cubby enrichment schedule',
				headline: 'The schedule nobody could find',
				paragraphs: [
					"Our kid's classes ended at a different time each day. New terms brought new timings, and make-up sessions went into pinned Telegram messages and emails. We mixed them up often enough to spend quite a bit of time waiting at the door.",
					"I put today's schedule on the dashboard, with make-up sessions marked. Pickup time was something we needed every day, so it belonged on the first screen.",
				],
			},
			{
				src: schedule,
				alt: 'Cubby schedule',
				headline: 'One plan for the whole household',
				paragraphs: [
					'We wanted to organize our schedule as a household: who drops our kid off at class, who picks him up, and who walks the dog. Those lived across Enrichment, Tasks and a few other places, so I abstracted them into Schedule and put them in one plan.',
					'Each responsibility has a usual owner, and any of us can claim it, ask for cover, or mark it done. A pickup linked to a class moves with that class, and anything that needs someone in the next couple of days shows up on Home.',
				],
			},
		],
	},
	{
		label: 'Health',
		note: 'four bodies, one of them a dog',
		intro:
			'Some records needed more detail than a date. A workout or an illness had to make sense when I came back to it later.',
		items: [
			{
				src: gymWorkout,
				alt: 'Cubby workouts',
				headline: 'What should I aim for today',
				paragraphs: [
					'At the gym I mostly went by whatever I remembered from the last session. On cable machines, that meant agar agar the position of the pin. I started logging weights, reps, rest times, and notes on failure or dropsets so I could check the previous set before the next one.',
					"There's also a lock screen for sweaty hands around buttons mid-workout. A new personal record posts to the journal, so it shows up alongside the other things we've been doing.",
				],
			},
			{
				src: illness,
				alt: 'Cubby illness log',
				headline: "One log when everyone's sick at once",
				paragraphs: [
					'Keeping a routine history helped, but illness needed a record of its own. Temperatures, doses, medicine timings, and MCs were scattered through Telegram, sometimes for several of us at once. By the consultation, the order of events was hard to reconstruct.',
					"I grouped the entries by illness episode so I could read them out to the doctor. It works for me, my wife, our kid, and the dog. Vet visits can be eight to twelve months apart, so I keep the vet's guidance with the episode and pull it up next time.",
				],
			},
			{
				src: sleep,
				alt: 'Cubby sleep',
				headline: 'How often, and how bad',
				paragraphs: [
					"When our kid started sleepwalking, we wanted to understand how severe it was and whether there was a pattern to it. I log each episode in one tap while it's happening, against the night it happened, and fill in sleep and wake times later.",
					'Looking across the weeks, we found that it usually happened when he was very tired, especially on days he skipped his nap. Having every episode in one place also meant we could pull it up when we spoke to the doctors.',
				],
			},
			{
				src: coffee,
				alt: 'Cubby coffee recipes',
				headline: 'Which brew actually tasted better',
				paragraphs: [
					'Coffee was a smaller version of the same memory problem. I could recall the last cup or two, so I started saving bean and water weights, timing, and clicks on my Kingrinder K6. A taste rating lets me compare what I actually liked across bags and roasters.',
				],
			},
		],
	},
	{
		label: 'Household',
		note: 'what comes in, runs out, and gets done',
		intro: 'The smaller things stayed too, because I kept running into them at home.',
		items: [
			{
				src: meals,
				alt: 'Cubby meals',
				headline: 'What did we eat yesterday?',
				paragraphs: [
					"We were just as bad at remembering meals, sometimes even what we'd had that afternoon. Now I log them at the table before we leave, with a photo, so I can look back at what we've eaten over the week.",
				],
			},
			{
				src: jobs,
				alt: 'Cubby jobs',
				headline: "A photo when the job's done",
				paragraphs: [
					"Jobs started as a tally of the chores I did around the house, like laundry or fixing a loose cabinet hinge. I modelled it on delivery apps, where the driver takes a photo once a job is complete, so each chore gets a photo when it's finished.",
					'It also turned out to be useful for recording the chores our kid did. Each job is credited to whoever did it, so when he sets the dinner table, it goes down as his.',
				],
			},
			{
				src: expiry,
				alt: 'Cubby expiry tracker',
				headline: 'The dates that actually matter',
				paragraphs: [
					"A friend mentioned her cosmetics expiring, which made me think about the milk and soy milk in our fridge. Cubby calculates the expiry from when I open something and how long it keeps. I'm more conscious of how long the milk has left now, even if I don't always keep to it.",
				],
			},
			{
				src: stock,
				alt: 'Cubby stock',
				headline: 'Before the last one runs out',
				paragraphs: [
					"Within a few weeks I forgot to buy NexGard Spectra and kibble for the dog, then my Pears soap and our toothpaste. Buying more at a time wasn't great either: NexGard isn't cheap, and a cupboard of spares can expire before we get through them.",
					"Now each supply has an order point, and every few weeks a stocktake prompts one of us to note what's left against it. Soap and toothpaste can just be marked as running low, because counting them would be a chore. Anything below its order point stays on Home and in the morning message until someone marks it ordered.",
				],
			},
			{
				src: market,
				alt: 'Cubby prices',
				headline: 'Which price is actually worth it',
				paragraphs: [
					"I had a rough sense of supermarket prices, but wine promotions and my Just Wine Club subscription made comparisons harder. I started recording prices by item, shop, and promotion, with unit prices to make different pack sizes comparable. The useful part is knowing which price I'd buy at again.",
				],
			},
		],
	},
	{
		label: 'Finance',
		note: 'the dates that cost money',
		intro: 'Remembering a date mattered rather more when missing it cost us money.',
		items: [
			{
				src: finance,
				alt: 'Cubby finance',
				headline: "The fee we couldn't waive",
				paragraphs: [
					"I'd missed credit card bills before and had the charges waived, but my wife's annual fee wasn't waived, and it was expensive. I put card fees, bills, and subscriptions together with their dates so we could check what was coming up.",
				],
			},
		],
	},
	{
		label: 'Life',
		note: 'the things worth keeping',
		intro: 'Once we were using Cubby for the everyday things, I brought in the moments I wanted to keep.',
		items: [
			{
				src: growingUp,
				alt: 'Cubby growing up',
				headline: 'The moments worth keeping',
				paragraphs: [
					"I'd tried recording things my wife did that I appreciated, along with things that frustrated me. I'd gone down a similar path on The Standup Podcast, and a complaint ledger about your spouse felt toxic.",
					'I turned it into a record for our kid: parent-teacher conferences, teacher feedback, naughty moments, and the sweet ones. Those were things I wanted us to remember.',
				],
			},
		],
	},
];

const heroScreenshots = [
	{
		full: dashboard,
		alt: "Cubby Home with the day's timeline and a Today list of expired food, due bills, an overdue stocktake and kibble running low",
		caption: 'Home: today',
	},
	{
		full: dashboardRecent,
		alt: "Cubby Home's quick log for coffee, meals, and prices, with recent meals and household jobs",
		caption: 'Home: quick log and recent',
	},
];

const stack = [
	{ role: 'Runtime', tools: 'Go' },
	{ role: 'Frontend', tools: 'SvelteKit, Tailwind' },
	{ role: 'Distribution', tools: 'Progressive Web App' },
	{ role: 'Data', tools: 'PostgreSQL' },
	{ role: 'Auth', tools: 'Stytch' },
	{ role: 'Observability', tools: 'Grafana Cloud, Umami, Uptime Kuma' },
];

const featureName = (alt: string) => {
	const name = alt.replace(/^Cubby /, '');
	return name.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + name.slice(1);
};
const featureId = (alt: string) =>
	`feature-${alt
		.replace(/^Cubby /, '')
		.toLowerCase()
		.replace(/\s+/g, '-')}`;
const groupId = (label: string) => (label === 'Household' ? 'home' : label.toLowerCase());

const heroStack = stack
	.filter((row) => ['Runtime', 'Frontend', 'Data', 'Distribution'].includes(row.role))
	.map((row) => row.tools)
	.join(' · ');

const index: IndexItem[] = [
	{ id: 'overview', number: '01', label: 'Overview' },
	{ id: 'home-design', number: '02', label: 'Home' },
	{
		id: 'decisions',
		number: '03',
		label: 'Decisions',
		children: [
			{ id: 'everyday-use', label: 'What I reach for every day' },
			{ id: 'domain-decisions', label: 'Where the data model stopped helping' },
			{ id: 'dashboard-design', label: 'Making room without making a mess' },
			{ id: 'visual-feedback', label: 'How it felt after repeated use' },
			{ id: 'another-user', label: 'Someone else has to want to use it' },
		],
	},
	{
		id: 'features',
		number: '04',
		label: 'Features',
		children: [
			...groups.map((group) => ({ id: groupId(group.label), label: group.label })),
			{ id: 'recap', label: 'Recap' },
		],
	},
	{ id: 'built-with', label: 'Built with' },
];
---

<div class="cubby-case-study text-base-content bg-base-200">
	<div class="bg-base-200">
		<div class="text-base-content/65 mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 text-sm">
			<div class="flex min-h-11 items-center justify-between lg:hidden">
				<a
					href="/#projects"
					class="hover:text-base-content inline-flex min-h-11 items-center gap-1.5 whitespace-nowrap">
					<Icon name="arrow-left" class="size-[1.2em]" /> Projects
				</a>
				<span>Cubby</span>
			</div>
			<nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="hidden min-h-11 items-center lg:flex">
				<ol class="flex items-center gap-1.5 whitespace-nowrap">
					<li>
						<a href="/#projects" class="hover:text-base-content inline-flex min-h-11 items-center whitespace-nowrap">
							Projects
						</a>
					</li>
					<li aria-hidden="true">
						<Icon name="chevron-right" class="size-[1.1em]" />
					</li>
					<li aria-current="page" class="text-base-content/85">
						Cubby
					</li>
				</ol>
			</nav>
		</div>
	</div>

	<div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-[11rem_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:gap-14">
		<aside class="hidden lg:block lg:pt-16">
			<nav
				data-case-study-section-nav
				aria-label="Sections"
				class="sticky top-8 max-h-[calc(100dvh-4rem)] overflow-y-auto pb-8">
				<p class="text-base-content/50 mb-4 text-xs tracking-wide">Contents</p>
				<CubbyIndex items={index} />
			</nav>
		</aside>

		<div class="min-w-0">
			<header class="pt-12 pb-14 lg:pt-16">
				<div>
					<div class="max-w-3xl">
						<img src={CubbyLogo.src} alt="Cubby" class="h-12" />
						<h1 class="mt-6 max-w-4xl text-4xl leading-[1.05] font-bold tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-5xl lg:text-6xl">
							It started with my towels and ended up managing our home.
						</h1>
						<p class="text-base-content/80 mt-6 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed">
							We kept medicine timings and class schedules in Telegram, and washing routines mostly in our heads. I
							started with a towel reminder and kept adding things we needed. Now a household of four and a dog uses
							Cubby.
						</p>
						<a
							href="https://cubby.dev"
							target="_blank"
							rel="noopener"
							class="cubby-visit hover:bg-base-100 mt-7 inline-flex items-center gap-2 border px-4 py-2 text-sm font-semibold whitespace-nowrap transition-colors focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2">
							Visit site <Icon name="external-link" class="size-[1.1em]" />
						</a>
						<p class="text-base-content/55 mt-5 text-sm">
							<span class="sr-only">Built with </span>
							{heroStack}
						</p>
					</div>
					<div
						style="view-transition-name: project-hero-cubby;"
						data-screenshot-gallery
						data-gallery-dialog-id="cubby-hero-dialog"
						class="mt-10 grid grid-cols-2 items-start gap-3 sm:mt-12 sm:gap-5">
						{heroScreenshots.map((shot, index) => (
							<button
								type="button"
								data-gallery-open={index}
								data-gallery-caption={shot.caption}
								class="group border-neutral/20 hover:border-neutral/45 bg-base-100 relative block w-full cursor-pointer overflow-hidden border transition-colors"
								aria-label={`Open full-size screenshot: ${shot.caption}`}>
								<Image
									src={shot.full}
									alt={shot.alt}
									width={645}
									densities={[1, 2]}
									loading="eager"
									class="block h-auto w-full"
								/>
								<span
									class="absolute right-2 bottom-2 grid size-9 place-items-center bg-neutral-950/85 text-white opacity-0 transition-opacity group-hover:opacity-100 group-focus-visible:opacity-100"
									aria-hidden="true">
									<Icon name="expand" class="size-4" />
								</span>
							</button>
						))}
						<ScreenshotDialog id="cubby-hero" images={heroScreenshots} />
					</div>
				</div>
			</header>

			<details data-case-study-section-nav class="case-study-contents group lg:hidden">
				<summary class="cubby-contents-summary focus-visible:outline-accent text-base-content/80 flex min-h-11 cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 border-y py-3 text-xs font-semibold tracking-wide focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2">
					<span>Contents</span>
					<Icon
						name="chevron-right"
						data-motion="spatial"
						class="size-4 shrink-0 transition-transform group-open:rotate-90"
					/>
				</summary>
				<nav aria-label="Sections" class="py-4">
					<CubbyIndex items={index} />
				</nav>
			</details>

			<section id="overview" aria-labelledby="overview-title" class="border-neutral/15 scroll-mt-20 lg:border-t">
				<div class="py-16 sm:py-24">
					<div class="grid gap-8 sm:grid-cols-[1fr_1.5fr] sm:gap-12">
						<h2 id="overview-title" class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
							A place for the things we keep forgetting.
						</h2>
						<div class="grid gap-5 leading-relaxed">
							<p>
								I designed and built Cubby around our household: recurring tasks, weekly limits, meals, health records,
								bills, and the moments I want to keep. It's a web app we can install on our phones.
							</p>
							<p>
								Home puts frequent actions and today's information within reach. Each feature keeps the detail it needs,
								while a shared recap brings the records back together.
							</p>
							<p>
								I keep changing it as I use it. Sometimes I've made a common action too troublesome, and sometimes a
								feature that works well for me turns out to be something my wife would rather leave to me.
							</p>
							<div class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-3 pt-2 font-semibold">
								<a
									href="#decisions"
									class="cubby-action hover:bg-base-100 inline-flex items-center gap-2 border px-4 py-2 text-sm whitespace-nowrap transition-colors">
									Read the product decisions <Icon name="chevron-right" class="size-4 shrink-0" />
								</a>
								<a
									href="#features"
									class="cubby-action hover:bg-base-100 inline-flex items-center gap-2 border px-4 py-2 text-sm whitespace-nowrap transition-colors">
									Explore the features <Icon name="chevron-right" class="size-4 shrink-0" />
								</a>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section id="home-design" aria-labelledby="home-title" class="border-neutral/15 scroll-mt-20 border-t">
				<CubbyHomeEvolution />
			</section>

			<section id="decisions" aria-labelledby="decisions-title" class="border-neutral/15 scroll-mt-20 border-t">
				<CubbyDecisions />
			</section>

			<section id="features" aria-labelledby="features-title" class="border-neutral/15 scroll-mt-20 border-t">
				<div class="pt-16 pb-4 sm:pt-24">
					<h2 id="features-title" class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
						What I built, and why it stayed
					</h2>
					<p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
						Each feature started with something we kept forgetting or struggling to find. These are the household
						problems behind the screens.
					</p>
				</div>

				{groups.map((group) => (
					<section id={groupId(group.label)} class="scroll-mt-20 pt-14">
						<div class="border-neutral/20 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-3 border-b pb-3">
							<h3 class="text-base-content/85 text-sm font-semibold tracking-[0.18em] uppercase">{group.label}</h3>
							<span class="text-base-content/50 text-xs">{group.note}</span>
						</div>
						<p class="text-base-content/80 mt-5 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">{group.intro}</p>
						<div data-screenshot-gallery data-gallery-dialog-id={`cubby-${groupId(group.label)}-dialog`}>
							<ul class="mt-8 grid grid-cols-2 gap-x-4 gap-y-6 sm:grid-cols-3 lg:grid-cols-5">
								{group.items.map((item, index) => (
									<li>
										<button
											type="button"
											data-gallery-open={index}
											data-gallery-caption={featureName(item.alt)}
											class="group border-neutral/20 hover:border-neutral/45 relative block w-full cursor-pointer overflow-hidden border transition-colors"
											aria-label={`Open full-size screenshot: ${featureName(item.alt)}`}>
											<Image
												src={item.src}
												alt={item.alt}
												width={230}
												densities={[1, 2, 3]}
												loading="lazy"
												decoding="async"
												class="block h-auto w-full"
											/>
											<span
												class="absolute right-1.5 bottom-1.5 grid size-8 place-items-center bg-neutral-950/85 text-white opacity-0 transition-opacity group-hover:opacity-100 group-focus-visible:opacity-100"
												aria-hidden="true">
												<Icon name="expand" class="size-4" />
											</span>
										</button>
										<a href={`#${featureId(item.alt)}`} class="cubby-feature-thumb mt-2 block text-sm font-semibold">
											{featureName(item.alt)}
										</a>
									</li>
								))}
							</ul>
							<ScreenshotDialog
								id={`cubby-${groupId(group.label)}`}
								images={group.items.map((item) => ({ full: item.src, alt: item.alt, caption: featureName(item.alt) }))}
							/>
						</div>
						<div class="border-neutral/15 mt-10 border-t">
							{group.items.map((item) => (
								<details id={featureId(item.alt)} class="cubby-feature group border-neutral/15 scroll-mt-20 border-b">
									<summary class="grid min-h-11 cursor-pointer list-none grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_auto] items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-1 py-4 sm:grid-cols-[8rem_minmax(0,1fr)_auto]">
										<span class="text-base-content/50 col-span-2 text-xs font-semibold tracking-wider uppercase sm:col-span-1">
											{featureName(item.alt)}
										</span>
										<h4 class="text-lg leading-snug font-bold sm:text-xl">{item.headline}</h4>
										<Icon name="chevron-right" class="size-4 shrink-0 transition-transform group-open:rotate-90" />
									</summary>
									<div class="text-base-content/85 grid max-w-2xl gap-4 pb-8 leading-relaxed sm:pl-36">
										{item.paragraphs.map((paragraph) => (
											<p>{paragraph}</p>
										))}
									</div>
								</details>
							))}
						</div>
					</section>
				))}

				<section id="recap" class="scroll-mt-20 py-16">
					<div class="grid items-start gap-10 sm:grid-cols-[auto_1fr] sm:gap-12">
						<div
							data-screenshot-gallery
							data-gallery-dialog-id="cubby-recap-dialog"
							class="mx-auto w-full max-w-[230px]">
							<button
								type="button"
								data-gallery-open="0"
								data-gallery-caption="Journal recap"
								class="group border-neutral/20 hover:border-neutral/45 relative block w-full cursor-pointer overflow-hidden border transition-colors"
